## [ISSUE] getGroupIdsWithViewPermission() returns authz resource IDs, not group IDs

**Severity:** HIGH -- V2 admins with an all-groups `view-members`/`manage-members` permission get an empty user list; per-group grants are never found

### The Problem

```java
resourceStore.findByType(server, AdminPermissionsSchema.GROUPS_RESOURCE_TYPE, groupResource -> {
    if (hasPermission(groupResource.getId(), AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW_MEMBERS, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE_MEMBERS)) {
        granted.add(groupResource.getId());
    }
});
```

Two things are wrong here:

1. **Which resources are found.** In V2, per-group resources are created by `AdminPermissionsSchema.getOrCreateResource()` with `resourceStore.create(resourceServer, name, clientId)` and no `setType(...)` call. The only resource with type `Groups` is the "all groups" resource that `AdminPermissionsSchema.init()` creates (`resource.setType(type)`, name `"Groups"`). So `findByType(server, "Groups", ...)` visits only that one resource.
2. **Which ID is used.** `groupResource.getId()` is the authorization resource's generated UUID. It is not a group ID. `hasPermission(String groupId, ...)` then calls `resourceStore.findByName(server, <resource uuid>)`, which returns null, so it falls back to the type resource. After that, `granted.add(groupResource.getId())` adds the UUID of the `Groups` resource.

Compare the V1 implementation this overrides (`GroupPermissions.getGroupIdsWithViewPermission`), which strips the prefix from the resource **name**: `granted.add(resource.getName().substring(RESOURCE_NAME_PREFIX.length()))`. Compare also `ClientPermissionsV2.getClientsWithPermission`, which adds `resource.getName()`. In V2 the resource name is the object ID (`resolveGroup` returns `group.getId()`).

### Theory / Proof

`UsersResource.searchForUser` (and `BruteForceUsersResource.searchForUser`) do:

```java
Set<String> groupIds = auth.groups().getGroupIdsWithViewPermission();
if (!groupIds.isEmpty()) session.setAttribute(UserModel.GROUPS, groupIds);
```

`JpaUserProvider.searchForUserStream` (~line 762) reads `UserModel.GROUPS` and adds `groupsWithPermissionsSubquery(...)`, which limits results to members of those group IDs. No group has the ID of the `Groups` authz resource, so the query returns zero rows.

### What happens today vs. what will break

- **Admin with an all-groups `view-members` permission and nothing else:** `canViewMembers(g)` is true for every group, but `GET /users` returns `[]`. The count endpoint uses the same method, so it returns 0 as well.
- **Admin with only per-group `view-members`:** this method returns an empty set, so there is no DB-side filter. `toRepresentation` then post-filters `canView` over the first `max` users of the whole realm. `testCanViewUserByViewGroupMembers` passes only because the test realm has two users. In a realm with more than `max` users, the page is short or empty and paging is wrong.

### Research

The V1 method and `ClientPermissionsV2.getClientsWithPermission` both map resource name to object ID. This is the only V2 evaluator that returns `Resource#getId()`.

### Recommendation

Map resource names back to group IDs. Also handle the all-groups case explicitly, because per-group resources carry no type:

```java
Set<String> granted = new HashSet<>();

// per-group resources: name == group id, no type set
resourceStore.findByResourceServer(server).forEach(r -> {
    if (AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.getResourceTypes().containsKey(r.getName())) return;
    if (session.groups().getGroupById(realm, r.getName()) != null
            && hasPermission(r.getName(), VIEW_MEMBERS, MANAGE_MEMBERS)) {
        granted.add(r.getName());
    }
});
```

Alternatively, set `resource.setType(resourceType)` in `AdminPermissionsSchema.getOrCreateResource` so that `findByType` finds per-group resources, and add `groupResource.getName()`. If the all-groups resource grants the scope, return empty (no restriction) instead of a bogus ID. Add a test that creates an all-groups `VIEW_MEMBERS` permission and asserts `users().search(...)` returns Alice.

## [ISSUE] `canManage()` accepts the `view` scope (privilege escalation)

**Severity:** HIGH -- a view-only admin passes `requireManage()` and can create groups and restructure the group tree

### The Problem

```java
@Override
public boolean canManage() {
    if (root.hasOneAdminRole(AdminRoles.MANAGE_USERS)) {
        return true;
    }

    return hasPermission(null, AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);
}
```

This body is a copy of `canView()`. `hasPermission` returns true if **any** expected scope is granted. A caller holding only an all-groups `view` permission therefore gets `canManage() == true`.

### Theory / Proof

`GroupsResource.addTopLevelGroup` (line 181) calls `auth.groups().requireManage()`, which delegates to `canManage()`, and then:
- with no `id`: `realm.createGroup(groupName)` creates a new top-level group.
- with an existing `id`: `realm.moveGroup(child, null)` moves **any** subgroup to the top level. There is no per-group `requireManage(child)` check.

The other V2 methods follow the right rule: `canManage(GroupModel)` checks only `MANAGE`, and the V1 `canManage()` checks only the `MANAGE_USERS` role. The new Javadoc in `GroupPermissionEvaluator#canManage()` ("For V2 only: Also if it has permission to VIEW or MANAGE groups") repeats the copy-paste.

### What happens today vs. what will break

`testViewGroups` grants all-groups `VIEW` but never tries to create a group, so the tests do not catch this. `testManageAllGroups` grants `MANAGE`, which passes either way. An admin who is meant to be read-only can now create groups and restructure the hierarchy.

### Research

`ClientPermissionsV2` and `UserPermissionsV2` require the `MANAGE` scope for manage checks and never accept `VIEW`.

### Recommendation

```java
@Override
public boolean canManage() {
    if (root.hasOneAdminRole(AdminRoles.MANAGE_USERS)) {
        return true;
    }

    return hasPermission(null, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);
}
```

Fix the Javadoc on `GroupPermissionEvaluator#canManage()` to match. Add a negative test: grant all-groups `VIEW` and assert that `groups().add(...)` returns 403.
